WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.250
Last time we got stuck.

2
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.715
A pinhole a quarter of a millimetre wide really does make a picture,

3
00:00:06.465 --> 00:00:09.875
but in the shade you were standing there counting to three,

4
00:00:09.625 --> 00:00:12.476
and by a window all the way to forty.

5
00:00:12.226 --> 00:00:18.846
Today your front plate gets one small piece of plastic: a lens, twelve millimetres across.

6
00:00:18.596 --> 00:00:22.738
If a hole already makes a picture, why does anyone need a lens?

7
00:00:23.467 --> 00:00:28.082
Ask most people what a lens does, and they'll tell you it makes things bigger.

8
00:00:27.832 --> 00:00:29.597
It magnifies.

9
00:00:29.347 --> 00:00:34.417
But the picture in this camera is always 36 by 24 millimetres.

10
00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:36.237
It's the size of the film.

11
00:00:35.987 --> 00:00:38.246
Nothing here needs to be any bigger.

12
00:00:38.967 --> 00:00:42.442
We wanted more light, so why not just make the hole bigger?

13
00:00:42.192 --> 00:00:44.277
Decide whether that works.

14
00:00:44.027 --> 00:00:49.519
Open it right up to the size of the large dot, 3.34 millimetres.

15
00:00:49.269 --> 00:00:51.122
And here's what you get:

16
00:00:50.872 --> 00:00:57.434
every single point of the tree now lands on the film as a disc 3.34 millimetres wide.

17
00:00:57.184 --> 00:00:59.764
The picture turns into a smear.

18
00:00:59.514 --> 00:01:01.249
More light, yes.

19
00:01:00.999 --> 00:01:03.078
But it's the mixed-up kind again.

20
00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:07.090
People were stuck on exactly this for a long time.

21
00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:08.038
Then in 1568,

22
00:01:07.788 --> 00:01:12.338
a man called Daniele Barbaro took a piece of glass that was

23
00:01:12.088 --> 00:01:16.566
made for spectacles and put it in the hole of a dark room.

24
00:01:16.316 --> 00:01:20.720
He slid a sheet of paper back and forth until the picture snapped sharp,

25
00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:24.241
and he noticed that a smaller opening made it clearer still.

26
00:01:23.991 --> 00:01:25.971
He could see that it worked.

27
00:01:25.721 --> 00:01:30.395
It took until 1611 for Johannes Kepler to work out why:

28
00:01:30.145 --> 00:01:32.950
how a lens bends light into a picture.

29
00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:38.242
Light from one point of the tree spreads out and covers the whole lens.

30
00:01:37.992 --> 00:01:41.337
The curved glass bends every one of those rays,

31
00:01:41.087 --> 00:01:46.144
and it bends the outer ones the most, so that they all come back together again.

32
00:01:45.894 --> 00:01:49.259
One point of the tree, one point on the film.

33
00:01:49.009 --> 00:01:52.429
And it's bright, because the whole lens collected it.

34
00:01:52.179 --> 00:01:55.419
Then the same thing happens for every other point.

35
00:01:55.169 --> 00:01:57.662
So a lens doesn't magnify.

36
00:01:57.412 --> 00:01:58.712
It gathers.

37
00:01:58.462 --> 00:02:02.032
But there's a catch: it only works at one distance.

38
00:02:01.782 --> 00:02:06.518
Slide the film forward or back, and every point swells into a disc again.

39
00:02:07.233 --> 00:02:09.888
A single lens has another problem.

40
00:02:09.638 --> 00:02:13.648
Points off to the side of the view come to a focus too soon.

41
00:02:13.398 --> 00:02:16.203
So the sharp picture doesn't lie flat.

42
00:02:15.953 --> 00:02:17.943
It lies on a curve.

43
00:02:17.693 --> 00:02:22.751
Put flat film behind it and the middle is sharp while the edges go soft.

44
00:02:22.501 --> 00:02:27.903
In 1812, William Wollaston bent a single lens into a shallow bowl,

45
00:02:27.653 --> 00:02:30.991
called a meniscus, with a stop in front of it.

46
00:02:30.741 --> 00:02:37.986
In 1840, Joseph Petzval designed a lens by pure calculation, the first time anyone had.

47
00:02:37.736 --> 00:02:39.196
It was bright.

48
00:02:38.946 --> 00:02:41.163
It was still soft at the edges.

49
00:02:41.867 --> 00:02:45.952
The usual fix is more glass, and more glass costs more.

50
00:02:45.702 --> 00:02:50.512
Your camera has exactly one lens, so it bends the film instead.

51
00:02:50.262 --> 00:02:56.275
The film lies on a curve with a radius of 32 millimetres, centred on the lens.

52
00:02:56.025 --> 00:02:58.995
That curve is where the camera gets its name.

53
00:02:58.745 --> 00:03:03.612
Now the film follows the sharp picture, from the middle out toward the ends.

54
00:03:03.362 --> 00:03:06.922
The lens is set for 3.7 metres,

55
00:03:06.672 --> 00:03:11.667
and at the large dot the middle is sharp from 1.5 metres to far away.

56
00:03:11.417 --> 00:03:15.972
Toward the ends it's a little softer, about like a single-use camera.

57
00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:17.885
Now the number.

58
00:03:17.635 --> 00:03:21.195
How much more light does the lens let in than the pinhole did?

59
00:03:20.945 --> 00:03:23.595
The pinhole is f/120.

60
00:03:23.345 --> 00:03:30.050
The lens, at its large dot, is f/9.6, which we call f/9.5.

61
00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:33.235
Light goes with one over the f-number, squared.

62
00:03:32.985 --> 00:03:35.475
So divide, then square.

63
00:03:35.225 --> 00:03:39.935
120 divided by 9.6 is 12.5, and 12.5 times 12.5 is about 156.

64
00:03:39.685 --> 00:03:42.760
Call it 150 times the light.

65
00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:50.226
That one-second count just turned into less than a hundredth of a second,

66
00:03:49.976 --> 00:03:54.726
which is far too short for anybody to count.

67
00:03:55.433 --> 00:03:57.708
In your kit, this is one step.

68
00:03:57.458 --> 00:04:03.328
The lens seats in the round pocket on the front plate, and three small ribs hold it there.

69
00:04:03.078 --> 00:04:08.788
It's one piece of plastic with a focal length of 32 millimetres,

70
00:04:08.538 --> 00:04:10.159
and it never moves: fixed focus.

71
00:04:09.909 --> 00:04:11.774
There's nothing to turn.

72
00:04:11.524 --> 00:04:16.754
The very first Kodak camera, in 1888, was fixed focus too.

73
00:04:16.504 --> 00:04:18.469
Point, press, done.

74
00:04:18.219 --> 00:04:20.464
Set the large dot for now.

75
00:04:20.214 --> 00:04:23.988
That quick blink of the rocker is a story for lesson five.

76
00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:26.190
So why a lens?

77
00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:31.545
A hole makes a picture by throwing light away, and a lens gathers it back to a point.

78
00:04:31.295 --> 00:04:33.200
You can see it at home.

79
00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:36.080
A glass ball, or a full glass of water,

80
00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:39.503
shows you the room upside down, because it's a lens.

81
00:04:39.253 --> 00:04:45.165
And you can try it: hold a magnifying glass between a window and a sheet of white paper,

82
00:04:44.915 --> 00:04:47.578
and move it until the window appears.

83
00:04:47.328 --> 00:04:49.908
But that leaves us with a new puzzle.

84
00:04:49.658 --> 00:04:54.175
That bright, sharp picture lands on the film for a fraction of a second,

85
00:04:53.925 --> 00:04:55.478
and then it's gone.

86
00:04:55.228 --> 00:04:59.653
So next time: how does a strip of plastic remember a picture?

87
00:04:59.403 --> 00:05:03.683
Every picture here is drawn from the model or simulated.

88
00:05:03.433 --> 00:05:06.656
No photograph has been taken with this camera yet.

